<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/19th-century">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Treaty · Peace</p><h1 class="enc-title">Treaty of Valençay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1813</span> · Part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Treaty of Valençay was signed on 11 December 1813 at the château of Valençay in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleon">Napoleon</a> held the deposed Ferdinand VII. Facing defeat in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peninsular-war">Peninsular War</a>, Napoleon recognised Ferdinand as King of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> in return for peace and the departure of British forces. The Spanish regency refused to ratify it, but Ferdinand was released and returned to Spain in March 1814.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/treaty-of-valencay.jpg" alt="Treaty of Valençay" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: fr:User:Valéry Beaud · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Chateau_Valencay_20050726.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
